(2620105, 356042, 27735, '<td valign="top">Hey Simon, if possible, you should post a photo of the business card here. I''d like to see how they turned out! Thanks for supporting my photography "habit".<br/> <br/><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by grandmarginal:</b></div><hr/><i> Really nice pic! I just downloaded it from shutterstock today and made business cards with it for the sales people at the boat store where I work! I did''nt even know it was from a dpc member!! </i></td></tr></table></td>', 'Hey Simon, if possible, you should post a photo of the business card here. I''d like to see how they turned out! Thanks for supporting my photography "habit". Originally posted by grandmarginal: Really nice pic! I just downloaded it from shutterstock today and made business cards with it for the sales people at the boat store where I work! I did''nt even know it was from a dpc member!! ', '2006-07-13 20:32:25.000000', NULL, 0),
(2620118, 354888, 44597, '<td valign="top">Thanks to those who voted.  I am very happy with the comment and with the result of my first entry into a DP Challenge.  21 out of 282 is an encouraging result.</td>', 'Thanks to those who voted.  I am very happy with the comment and with the result of my first entry into a DP Challenge.  21 out of 282 is an encouraging result.', '2006-07-13 20:38:18.000000', NULL, 0),
(2620705, 358619, 11854, '<td valign="top">Trading post...<br/>   An under rated shot! I gave this an 8 in voting. I liked the starkness of the posts against the dramatic sky. I really loved the small red/orange flowers at the bottom of the posts amongst the dead/dying grass. Great find!</td>', 'Trading post...   An under rated shot! I gave this an 8 in voting. I liked the starkness of the posts against the dramatic sky. I really loved the small red/orange flowers at the bottom of the posts amongst the dead/dying grass. Great find!', '2006-07-13 23:38:38.000000', NULL, 0),
(2620778, 358619, 53147, '<td valign="top">Trading post - The posts surely arent moving now - but who knows when that storm comes through. Easily met the challenge and the contrast between the posts andthe sky is nice. But my eyes are really drawn to the clouds. They are the focal point for me - and they are cool. Would be nice to see another version of this with a nice blue sky with poofy clouds. Good focus and detasil through out and the lighting onthe foregournd is really quite good considering the sky in the background. Top 50 out of almost 300 is great. Good work Deb.</td>', 'Trading post - The posts surely arent moving now - but who knows when that storm comes through. Easily met the challenge and the contrast between the posts andthe sky is nice. But my eyes are really drawn to the clouds. They are the focal point for me - and they are cool. Would be nice to see another version of this with a nice blue sky with poofy clouds. Good focus and detasil through out and the lighting onthe foregournd is really quite good considering the sky in the background. Top 50 out of almost 300 is great. Good work Deb.', '2006-07-13 23:53:35.000000', NULL, 0),
(2620849, 356700, 22975, '<td valign="top">Good job on getting good lighting on this... could have been a difficult subject with all the rust, but you managed to keep in the details.  Good job!</td>', 'Good job on getting good lighting on this... could have been a difficult subject with all the rust, but you managed to keep in the details.  Good job!', '2006-07-14 00:07:51.000000', NULL, 0),
(2621396, 357436, 41337, '<td valign="top">I don''t think I could have found something more stationary than this so kudos on choosing a strong subject for the challenge. <br/> <br/>I think the problem voters had with this image was it looked too much  like a snapshot. Almost any shot like this has that potiental so I think you really need to try and frame it in a way that is not so common. Here''s how I think I would have approached it: <br/> <br/>My first thought would be to stand directly in front of the truck and get a close up of the weeds and see how that looked in the viewfinder. Then I''d probably zoom out a bit to get enough of the car so that it''s identifiable as a truck with weeds growing out of it. Another idea would be getting right up to the bumper and holding the camera as high up as I could with the widest lens possible and get a top view of the front of the truck. Basically, I''d just be looking for interesting ways to frame this but also limit any hint of movement within the frame. In your photo there is some level of that because the truck is seen as if it was entering the frame from the right. Even though we know it''s not moving subconciously I read it as such (from left to right) that''s why choosing a unique composition (maybe just using portrait style) for this would have been golden, IMO. <br/> <br/>Anyway, I''m not sure this all makes sense but I hope you get the gist of what I''m saying. Let me end this by saying your image does look good. It''s nicely sharpened, has good color and tonal range. I think you''ll be doing much better in these challenges soon. Keep up the good work!<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2006-07-14 03:04:02.</i></td>', 'I don''t think I could have found something more stationary than this so kudos on choosing a strong subject for the challenge.  I think the problem voters had with this image was it looked too much  like a snapshot. Almost any shot like this has that potiental so I think you really need to try and frame it in a way that is not so common. Here''s how I think I would have approached it:  My first thought would be to stand directly in front of the truck and get a close up of the weeds and see how that looked in the viewfinder. Then I''d probably zoom out a bit to get enough of the car so that it''s identifiable as a truck with weeds growing out of it. Another idea would be getting right up to the bumper and holding the camera as high up as I could with the widest lens possible and get a top view of the front of the truck. Basically, I''d just be looking for interesting ways to frame this but also limit any hint of movement within the frame. In your photo there is some level of that because the truck is seen as if it was entering the frame from the right. Even though we know it''s not moving subconciously I read it as such (from left to right) that''s why choosing a unique composition (maybe just using portrait style) for this would have been golden, IMO.  Anyway, I''m not sure this all makes sense but I hope you get the gist of what I''m saying. Let me end this by saying your image does look good. It''s nicely sharpened, has good color and tonal range. I think you''ll be doing much better in these challenges soon. Keep up the good work!', '2006-07-14 04:02:44.000000', '2006-07-14 04:04:02.000000', 0),
(2621407, 357505, 53964, '<td valign="top">Okay, I imagine there''s technical terms, but I don''t know them!<br/>I love the diagonal division, with bright-on-dark in one, grey-on-grey in the other. And the lines within lines within lines, almost like those puzzles where you count the number of triangles. <br/>Very crisp :) <br/>(And anyone living in our area would understand about the earthquake thing ;) )</td>', 'Okay, I imagine there''s technical terms, but I don''t know them!I love the diagonal division, with bright-on-dark in one, grey-on-grey in the other. And the lines within lines within lines, almost like those puzzles where you count the number of triangles. Very crisp :) (And anyone living in our area would understand about the earthquake thing ;) )', '2006-07-14 04:09:32.000000', NULL, 0),
